    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The Point Reyes watershed system is valuable habitat for the endangered Coho Salmon, a keystone species in this fragile ecosystem. The current management paradigm treats the entire Point Reyes peninsula as a single conservation unit in which habitat needs to only be maintained at the watershed level. However, the validity of a multi-creek meta-population model, in which the creek populations are not separate, for this system is not well verified. Previous work has relied on the synthesis of fragmented data from studies with alternative goals in order to inform policy, while this approach introduces genetic evidence as a more quantitative, direct method of determining population makeup and conservation strategy. Through PCR amplification and DNA sequencing, this study genotypes 128 fish from a collection of tissue samples for a suite of single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) that will provide a quantitative basis for assessing the relationship between various stream populations. The individuals selected for this project represent five distinct geographic populations, which are genotyped for SNPs occurring in introns, exons, and the major histocompatibility complex coding region, creating a diverse profile for each individual. Additionally, due to the amount of temporal variability in the tissue collection date, the genotype profiles created from this assay are also applied to temporal comparisons. The discovery of significant similarities in SNP distributions between streams supports the current conservation paradigm. The data presented here provides a template for genetic stock identification projects in similar coastal watershed systems and introduces a tool to supplement tag data in tracking endangered salmonid populations.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
